@meta
id: 22-16
title: Ragged lines, one append
chapter: 22
type: lesson
skills: visual-block, block-append
par: 34
minutes: 5
vscode: Alt+click at the end of forty lines, or a regex replace
open: orders.csv

@brief
The last lesson's block worked because the column was aligned. Most
lines are not aligned, and `A` on a block that stops short of a line's
end will pad the line with spaces to reach the block. For "append to
the end of every line" there is a special case: `$` inside a block
selection extends the block to the end of *each* line, whatever its
length, and `A` then appends after each one's own last character.

Forty rows in the export need a currency. Append `,GBP` to every data
row and `,currency` to the header line.

Forty lines is too many to count `j` for. Build the block from the
first data row to the last with `G`, or with a search for the last
order id; a block can be extended by any motion, the same as the other
two visual modes.

@keys
| `$` | in block mode, extend the block to the end of every line |
| `A` | append after the end of each line |
| `G` | extend the block to the last line |
| `/pattern` | extend the block to a match |

@hints
Skip the header with `j`. `Ctrl-V`, then `G` takes the block to the
last row without a count.
--
Now `$`. Watch the highlight change from a rectangle to a ragged right
edge. `A`, `,GBP`, `Esc`.
--
`j`, `Ctrl-V`, `G`, `$`, `A,GBP`, `Esc`. Then `gg`, `A,currency`, `Esc`
for the header.

@solution
    j Ctrl-V G $ A ,GBP <Esc> g g A ,currency <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twenty-seven keystrokes. The order matters: `$` after the block has
its height. `$` sets a flag on the block rather than a column, and a
`G` pressed after it puts a column back, so the flag is lost and `A`
pads to that column instead. Height first, then `$`, then `A`.

`:'<,'>s/$/,GBP/` and `:'<,'>normal A,GBP` are both honest alternatives
and both longer. Block `$A` is the shortest way vim has to say "at the
end of each of these lines", and it comes up constantly: trailing
commas, semicolons, closing tags, a `\` at the end of every line of a
shell command.

